# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
# Coverage artifacts. `.coverage` is a SQLite database, and it appears untracked
# and UNIGNORED after the first test run that produces one - exactly the shape a
# broad `git add` sweeps in. The committed-file-type gate refuses it too, but
# that gate is the backstop and this is the primary layer.
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
# `coverage.json` is what `docs/reviews/check-coverage-floors.py` reads, so it
# appears the moment that gate is turned on. Listed here BEFORE it exists: an
# artefact list beside a tool is blind to the format nobody added.
coverage.json
htmlcov/
.mypy_cache/

# Environment / secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key

# Local data
*.db
*.sqlite3

# Vendored third-party source documents. Research CITES and links sources; it
# does not redistribute them. A confidential vendor PDF and an unlicensed RAML
# reached two public remotes on the project this template came from.
#
# SCOPED TO THE WHOLE REPOSITORY, not to docs/research/. The mechanism is a
# broad `git add` sweeping files nobody intended to stage, and THE REPOSITORY
# ROOT - where such a sweep picks a file up - is exactly where a path-scoped
# block does not apply.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, stated rather than assumed: a legitimate PDF someone later
# wants in the repository is blocked too. The answer then is `git add -f` PLUS
# an entry in `.file-type-allowlist`, because check-committed-file-types.py
# denylists these extensions independently and will still refuse it.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE CONTROL. `.gitignore` is bypassed by `git add -f` and does
# nothing about a file that is already tracked. It removes the accident, not
# the intent. The gate is the control.
*.pdf
*.raml
*.doc
*.docx
*.ppt
*.pptx
*.xls
*.xlsx
*.zip

# Credential material the standard names explicitly
*.pem
secrets/
